[cot:thought] The August 2026 update angle is now clear. The prior post covered
[cot:thought] Gartner and IEA June 2026 data. The new vintage is IEA Electricity Mid-Year Update 2026 and LBNL Queued Up 2026. Global electricity demand growth has accelerated to 3.6% in 2026 and 3.8% in 2027. Data centers are now flagged as a structural driver.
[cot:thought] The headline will highlight a +26% increase to 565 TWh. US demand growth is
[cot:thought] now restated at 2.6% in 2025 and roughly 1% in H1 2026. Services demand rose by 3%, with data centers as the main driver. The full-year forecast is 1.8% in 2026 and 3% in 2027. US wholesale prices largely avoided the LNG/Hormuz price shock that hit Europe and Japan. This supports onsite gas economics and AI power cost geography.
[cot:thought] US fossil spending
[cot:thought] on data center gas turbines is projected to outpace China in 2026 for the first time in decades. Flexibility and negative pricing are rising across regions: South Australia and California average roughly 20% of hours at negative prices, while Spain sees 17%.
[cot:thought] European heatwaves produced USD 600/MWh price spreads, elevating battery and demand-response value. Renewables are now expected to overtake coal in 2026, with solar adding approximately 600 TWh. Grid flexibility becomes critical as these trends unfold.
[cot:thought] LBNL Queued Up 2026
[cot:thought] reports 2,061 GW total active queues. Generation is 1,312 GW and storage is 749 GW. This is down 10% year-over-year.
[cot:thought] Gas in queue grew 86% to 253 GW. The IA backlog is 549 GW. Median interconnection-to-commissioning time exceeds five years.
[cot:thought] The headline will pair this with flat US power prices during the Hormuz shock. The carried Gartner and IEA dual ledger will anchor the narrative.
